#: ../../../topics/security.rst:5
msgid "Security"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:7
msgid ""
"Scrapy defaults are optimized for web scraping, not for the security posture "
"that you might expect from software that handles untrusted input or runs in "
"a shared or exposed environment. Some common security practices are "
"unnecessary for many scraping use cases, and a few can even prevent valid "
"ones (for example, sites that you must scrape may use misconfigured TLS "
"certificates or serve content over unencrypted protocols)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:14
msgid ""
"This page highlights the Scrapy defaults that have security implications, so "
"that you can make an informed decision about whether to keep them, and "
"explains how to harden them along with the trade-offs involved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:20
msgid ""
"None of the options below are silver bullets. Which of them make sense "
"depends on your threat model: whether the URLs you crawl come from trusted "
"sources, whether the machine running Scrapy is exposed to a network you do "
"not control, whether the data you handle is sensitive, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:28
msgid "Treat responses as untrusted input"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:30
msgid ""
"Regardless of any setting, remember that response data comes from servers "
"you do not control, even when you trust the site you are crawling, as "
"responses may be tampered with in transit or the server itself may be "
"compromised."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:34
msgid ""
"Never pass response data to functions that can execute code or otherwise act "
"on their input in an unsafe way, such as :func:`eval`, :func:`exec`, or :"
"func:`pickle.loads`, and be careful when writing response data to paths "
"derived from the response itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:42
msgid "Memory use when parsing responses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:44
msgid ""
"Parsing a response with :ref:`selectors <topics-selectors>` builds an in-"
"memory tree of the whole response body, which takes several times as much "
"memory as the body itself. Scrapy parses without the size limits that "
"libxml2 applies by default, so the size of that tree is bound only by the "
"size of the response, as controlled by :setting:`DOWNLOAD_MAXSIZE` (default: "
"1 GiB)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:50
msgid ""
"XML entities are left unresolved, so the tree stays proportional to the "
"response body even for input crafted as an `XML bomb <https://lxml.de/FAQ."
"html#is-lxml-vulnerable-to-xml-bombs>`_. A server can still make a crawler "
"allocate a lot of memory by returning a very large response, though, so if "
"you know the size of the responses you care about, lower the limit:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:57
msgid "DOWNLOAD_MAXSIZE = 32 * 1024 * 1024  # 32 MiB"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:61
msgid ""
"**Pro:** a server cannot make the crawler allocate more memory than the "
"limit allows, whether by returning a large response or by crafting one that "
"is expensive to parse."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:65
msgid ""
"**Con:** you can no longer scrape sites that legitimately serve responses "
"above the limit, as those responses are dropped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:71
msgid "Parser limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:73
msgid ""
"The limits that libxml2 applies by default, such as 256 nesting levels and "
"10 MB per text node, can be restored by overriding :attr:`~scrapy.http."
"TextResponse.selector` in a response subclass and swapping responses in a :"
"ref:`downloader middleware <topics-downloader-middleware>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:78
msgid ""
"from functools import cached_property\n"
"\n"
"from scrapy import Selector\n"
"from scrapy.http import HtmlResponse\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class LimitedHtmlResponse(HtmlResponse):\n"
"    @cached_property\n"
"    def selector(self):\n"
"        return Selector(self, huge_tree=False)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class LimitedParsingMiddleware:\n"
"    def process_response(self, request, response, spider):\n"
"        if isinstance(response, HtmlResponse):\n"
"            return response.replace(cls=LimitedHtmlResponse)\n"
"        return response"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:98
msgid ""
"Do the same with :class:`~scrapy.http.XmlResponse` if you also parse XML."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:100
msgid ""
"These limits apply per node, so :setting:`DOWNLOAD_MAXSIZE` remains your "
"bound on total memory: a response made of many small elements is parsed in "
"full and uses as much memory either way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:104
msgid ""
"**Pro:** deeply nested responses, and responses with very large individual "
"nodes, become cheaper to parse."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:107
msgid ""
"**Con:** parsing stops at those limits without raising, so a legitimate page "
"that exceeds them yields incomplete data and no error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:111
msgid "TLS connections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:116
msgid "Certificate verification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:118
msgid ""
"By default Scrapy does **not** verify the TLS certificate of HTTPS servers, "
"as controlled by the :setting:`DOWNLOAD_VERIFY_CERTIFICATES` setting "
"(default: ``False``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:122
msgid ""
"This default favors reach over security: many sites that are otherwise fine "
"to scrape have expired, self-signed, or otherwise invalid certificates, and "
"verifying certificates would make requests to them fail."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:126
msgid ""
"If the integrity of the connection matters to you (for example, to detect "
"man-in-the-middle attacks), set:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:129
msgid "DOWNLOAD_VERIFY_CERTIFICATES = True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:133
msgid ""
"**Pro:** requests to servers with invalid or untrusted certificates fail "
"instead of silently succeeding, protecting you from some man-in-the-middle "
"attacks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:137
msgid ""
"**Con:** you can no longer scrape sites with misconfigured certificates "
"without re-disabling verification for them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:143
msgid "Protocol versions and ciphers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:145
msgid ""
"You can restrict the TLS protocol versions that Scrapy accepts through the :"
"setting:`DOWNLOAD_TLS_MIN_VERSION` and :setting:`DOWNLOAD_TLS_MAX_VERSION` "
"settings, e.g. to reject obsolete protocol versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:149
msgid ""
"By default Scrapy uses the OpenSSL ``DEFAULT`` cipher list (:setting:"
"`DOWNLOADER_CLIENT_TLS_CIPHERS`), which favors compatibility and still "
"allows some older, weaker ciphers. Set it to ``None`` to instead use the "
"curated cipher list of the underlying TLS implementation (Twisted), which "
"excludes weak ciphers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:155
msgid "DOWNLOADER_CLIENT_TLS_CIPHERS = None"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:159
msgid ""
"**Pro:** connections that would negotiate a weak cipher fail instead of "
"succeeding."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:162
msgid ""
"**Con:** you can no longer connect to servers that only support the excluded "
"ciphers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:168
msgid "Unencrypted protocols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:170
msgid ""
"By default Scrapy enables download handlers for unencrypted protocols, "
"namely ``http://`` and ``ftp://`` (see :setting:`DOWNLOAD_HANDLERS_BASE`). "
"Data sent and received over these protocols, including any credentials, "
"travels in plain text and can be read or modified by anyone on the network "
"path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:175
msgid ""
"If you only crawl over encrypted protocols, you can disable the unencrypted "
"ones so that no request can accidentally be sent unencrypted:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:178
msgid ""
"DOWNLOAD_HANDLERS = {\n"
"    \"http\": None,\n"
"    \"ftp\": None,\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:185
msgid ""
"**Pro:** a misconfigured or maliciously-redirected request cannot leak data "
"over an unencrypted connection, as such requests fail instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:188
msgid ""
"**Con:** you can no longer crawl resources that are only available over "
"those protocols."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:191
msgid ""
"Note that disabling the ``http`` handler also prevents plain-HTTP requests "
"that result from following an ``http://`` redirect or link, which is often "
"the point of disabling it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:198
msgid "Local and non-network resources"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:200
msgid ""
"By default Scrapy enables download handlers for the ``file://`` and ``data:"
"`` schemes (see :setting:`DOWNLOAD_HANDLERS_BASE`). The ``file://`` handler "
"reads arbitrary files from the local filesystem, limited only by the "
"permissions of the process running Scrapy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:205
msgid ""
"This is convenient (for example, to parse a local HTML file), but it is a "
"risk if any of the URLs you schedule come from an untrusted source: a "
"crafted ``file:///etc/passwd`` URL could read local files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:209
msgid "If you do not need them, disable these handlers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:211
msgid ""
"DOWNLOAD_HANDLERS = {\n"
"    \"file\": None,\n"
"    \"data\": None,\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:218
msgid ""
"**Pro:** crawled URLs cannot be used to read local files or inline data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:220
msgid "**Con:** you can no longer fetch ``file://`` or ``data:`` URLs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:222
msgid ""
"More generally, if you crawl URLs from untrusted sources, consider "
"validating their schemes (and, where applicable, their hosts) before "
"scheduling requests, to avoid server-side request forgery (SSRF) and similar "
"issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:229
msgid "Telnet console"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:231
msgid ""
"Scrapy enables the :ref:`telnet console <topics-telnetconsole>` by default (:"
"setting:`TELNETCONSOLE_ENABLED`). The telnet console is a Python shell "
"running inside the Scrapy process, so anyone who can connect to it can run "
"arbitrary code in that process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:236
msgid ""
"By default the console binds to ``127.0.0.1`` (:setting:"
"`TELNETCONSOLE_HOST`) and is protected by a username (:setting:"
"`TELNETCONSOLE_USERNAME`, default ``scrapy``) and an automatically generated "
"password (:setting:`TELNETCONSOLE_PASSWORD`), so it is only reachable from "
"the local machine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:244
msgid ""
"Telnet does not provide any transport-layer security, so the username/"
"password authentication does not protect the credentials or the session from "
"anyone able to observe the traffic. Never expose the telnet console over an "
"untrusted network by changing :setting:`TELNETCONSOLE_HOST` to a non-local "
"address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:250
msgid "If you do not use the telnet console, disable it entirely:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:252
msgid "TELNETCONSOLE_ENABLED = False"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:256
msgid ""
"**Pro:** removes a local code-execution surface and one less listening port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:258
msgid ""
"**Con:** you can no longer :ref:`inspect and control a running crawler "
"<topics-telnetconsole>` through it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:264
msgid "Credential leakage across domains"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:266
msgid ""
"Some Scrapy features attach credentials or other sensitive headers to "
"requests, and a crawl that spans multiple domains can leak them to "
"unintended hosts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:269
msgid ""
"HTTP authentication credentials set through :class:`~scrapy."
"downloadermiddlewares.httpauth.HttpAuthMiddleware` are only sent to the "
"domain set in :setting:`HTTPAUTH_DOMAIN`. Leave this set to the intended "
"domain rather than ``None`` so that credentials are not sent to every domain "
"you crawl."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/security.rst:275
msgid ""
"The ``Referer`` header may disclose the URLs you crawl to other sites. The "
"default :setting:`REFERRER_POLICY` already avoids sending the referrer from "
"HTTPS to HTTP, but you can tighten it further (for example, to ``same-"
"origin`` or ``no-referrer``) if needed."
msgstr ""
